{
  "id": "80618",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Types of Radioactive Decay",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "yytq5joc5e4l527",
                "text": "Radioactive Decay"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2qla9gwzyga0f0k",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Following the somewhat serendipitous discovery of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "radioactivity"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The spontaneous emission of particles or electromagnetic radiation from the unstable nucleus of an atom as it transforms into a more stable form. This process can involve the emission of alpha particles, beta particles, or gamma rays."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4120779344",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3607796214",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " by Henri Becquerel, many prominent scientists began to investigate this new, intriguing phenomenon. Among them were Marie Curie (the first woman to win a Nobel prize and the only person to win two Nobel prizes in different sciences—chemistry, and physics), who was the first to coin the term "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "radioactivity"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and Ernest Rutherford (of gold foil experiment fame), who investigated and named three of the most common types of radiation. In the early 20th century, many radioactive substances were discovered, the properties of radiation were investigated and quantified, and a solid understanding of radiation and "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "radioactive decay"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The spontaneous process by which an unstable atomic nucleus loses energy by emitting radiation in the form of particles or electromagnetic waves. This can result in the transformation of the original atom into a different element or a different isotope of the same element."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3464638190",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1958993530",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " was developed."
              }
            ],
            "id": "h7bg4dyvy7q84pq",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The spontaneous change of an unstable nuclide into another is radioactive decay. The unstable nuclide is called the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "parent nuclide"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The original unstable nuclide that undergoes radioactive decay to form a different nuclide, known as the daughter nuclide."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3025334716",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2689587365",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": "; the nuclide that results from the decay is known as the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "daughter nuclide"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The nuclide that is produced as a result of the radioactive decay of a parent nuclide. It may itself be stable or may undergo further radioactive decay."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "202748168",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1904723642",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ". The daughter nuclide may be stable, or it may decay itself. The radiation produced during radioactive decay is such that the daughter nuclide lies closer to the band of stability than the parent nuclide, so the location of a nuclide relative to the band of stability can serve as a guide to the kind of decay it will undergo (shown below)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "au1il7syzbw2kb1",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A diagram shows two spheres composed of many smaller white and green spheres connected by a right-facing arrow with another, down-facing arrow coming off of it. The left sphere, labeled “Parent nucleus uranium dash 238” has two white and two green spheres that are near one another and are outlined in red. These two green and two white spheres are shown near the tip of the down-facing arrow and labeled “alpha particle.” The right sphere, labeled “Daughter nucleus radon dash 234,” looks the same as the left, but has a space for four smaller spheres outlined with a red dotted line.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "A nucleus of uranium-238 (the parent nuclide) undergoes"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      }
                    ],
                    "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                    "src": "\\alpha",
                    "subtype": "latex",
                    "type": "formula_inline"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "decay to form thorium-234 (the daughter nuclide). The alpha particle removes two protons (green) and two neutrons (gray) from the uranium-238 nucleus."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "t1iaiihsu51654m",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2jbn6qzssii8jso",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/f3/f3935c7355bc0d08f4ad2f8842022e2f/parent_daughter.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 600
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "After the discovery of radioactivity, the field of nuclear chemistry was created and developed rapidly during the early 20th century. A slew of discoveries in the 1930s and 1940s, along with World War II, combined to usher in the nuclear age in the mid-20th century. Scientists learned how to create new substances, and certain isotopes of certain elements were found to possess the capacity to produce unprecedented amounts of energy, with the potential to cause tremendous damage during war, as well as produce enormous amounts of power for society’s needs during peace."
              }
            ],
            "id": "c3k3847n07tqy7h",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "as2yneikj6ov6a7",
                "text": "Types of Radioactive Decay"
              }
            ],
            "id": "orukv64o4eynh10",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Rutherford’s experiments involving the interaction of radiation with a magnetic or electric field (shown below) helped him determine that one type of radiation consisted of positively charged and relatively massive "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particles. A second type was made up of negatively charged and much less massive "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "10435212",
                "src": "\\beta",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particles, and a third was uncharged electromagnetic waves, gamma ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\gamma",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": ") rays. We now know that "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particles are high-energy helium nuclei, "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2986846530",
                "src": "\\beta",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particles are high-energy electrons, and "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\gamma",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " radiation composes high-energy electromagnetic radiation. We classify different types of radioactive decay by the radiation produced."
              }
            ],
            "id": "03vxlfo5of4djnn",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "A diagram is shown. A gray box on the left side of the diagram labeled “Lead block” has a chamber hollowed out in the center in which a sample labeled “Radioactive substance” is placed. A blue beam is coming from the sample, out of the block, and passing through two horizontally placed plates that are labeled “Electrically charged plates.” The top plate is labeled with a positive sign while the bottom plate is labeled with a negative sign. The beam is shown to break into three beams as it passes in between the plates; in order from top to bottom, they are red, labeled “beta rays,” purple labeled “gamma rays” and green labeled “alpha rays.” The beams are shown to hit a vertical plate labeled “Photographic plate” on the far right side of the diagram.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Alpha particles, which are attracted to the negative plate and deflected by a relatively small amount, must be positively charged and relatively massive. Beta particles, which are attracted to the positive plate and deflected a relatively large amount, must be negatively charged and relatively light. Gamma rays, which are unaffected by the electric field, must be uncharged."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "cxbffaj4sy7h5v4",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9uaut3fgcudvh3p",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/4e/4e6d6d41cd4cedc70275de677975fda4/radioactive_mirror.jfif",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 600
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "45iy8jro1xu0ewu",
                "text": "Alpha Decay"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ugbgjwezv2cg3nc",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Alpha decay"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A type of radioactive decay in which an unstable nucleus emits an alpha particle (consisting of two protons and two neutrons) and transforms into a new nucleus with a lower atomic number and mass number."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "954980659",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4144342113",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is the emission of an "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particle from the nucleus. For example, polonium-210 undergoes "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " decay:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "9kfw5r41f6jydkd",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ctsrgy8yltkkjnt",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "r3cimjdtgkbvdz9",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_{84}^{210}\\mathrm{Po}\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_2^4\\mathrm{He}\\;+\\;{}_{82}^{206}\\mathrm{Pb}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{or}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;{}_{84}^{210}\\mathrm{Po}\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_2^4\\alpha\\;+\\;{}_{82}^{206}\\mathrm{Pb}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Alpha decay occurs primarily in heavy nuclei (A > 200, Z > 83). Because the loss of an "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\alpha",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " particle gives a daughter nuclide with a mass number four units smaller and an atomic number two units smaller than those of the parent nuclide, the daughter nuclide has a slightly larger n:p ratio than the parent nuclide. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "1efov49vcdr3ltb",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "byqnp1topt0pmdp",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "m16lt6s4k001eky",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/26/2682eb2c7b209e2fa7cee67416ebbad0/alpha_decay.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 550
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "769365083",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "1054705467",
