      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "897yr1mvy4pq8ao",
                "text": "Positron Emission"
              }
            ],
            "id": "7458tzl40zc3emd",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Positron emission"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A type of beta decay in which an unstable nucleus emits a positron (the antimatter counterpart of an electron) and a neutrino. This process occurs when a proton in the nucleus is converted into a neutron. Positron emission decreases the atomic number by 1 while the mass number remains unchanged."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "454100572",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "260243070",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " ("
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": ""
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": false,
                "src": "\\beta^+",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": " decay) is the emission of a positron from the nucleus. Oxygen-15 is an example of a nuclide that undergoes positron emission:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "l1gatze4299bf28",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "kefr6y4f0vkpqzv",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "n5nges0iyfpyyh8",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_8^{15}\\mathrm O\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_{+1}^0\\mathrm e\\;+\\;{}_7^{15}\\mathrm N\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\mathrm{or}\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;{}_8^{15}\\mathrm O\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_{+1}^0\\mathrm\\beta\\;+\\;{}_7^{15}\\mathrm N",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Positron emission is observed for proton-rich nuclides (low n:p ratio). These nuclides lie below the band of stability. Positron decay is the conversion of a proton into a neutron with the emission of a positron. The n:p ratio increases, and the daughter nuclide lies closer to the band of stability than the parent nuclide."
              }
            ],
            "id": "5j2l1fdfu4neqf0",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "wby3etzao8g9tn1",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ax9tgvb4e9poagg",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/d3/d3b89ace9bfb1112d69d271f9b91c129/positron.png",
            "type": "img",
