      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "s78b7f2kxq79djw",
                "text": "Electron Capture"
              }
            ],
            "id": "dav51v49r9nshe3",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Electron capture"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A process in which an unstable nucleus captures an inner orbital electron and combines it with a proton to form a neutron and a neutrino. This decreases the atomic number by 1 without changing the mass number, often leaving the daughter nuclide in an excited state."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "4138728689",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4002229500",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " occurs when one of the inner electrons in an atom is captured by the atom’s nucleus. For example, potassium-40 undergoes electron capture:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "tbja0vwao8bxb90",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "rnzrng28avhsbf2",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "0h4oxds0g09ul8h",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{}_{19}^{40}\\mathrm K\\;+\\;{}_{-1}^0\\mathrm e\\;\\rightarrow\\;{}_{18}^{40}\\mathrm{Ar}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "In this process, an inner shell electron combines with a proton and is converted into a neutron. The loss of an inner shell electron leaves a vacancy that is filled by one of the outer electrons. As the outer electron drops into the vacancy, it emits energy. In most cases, the energy emitted is in the form of an X-ray. Like positron emission, electron capture occurs for proton-rich nuclei that lie below the band of stability. Electron capture has the same effect on the nucleus as does positron emission: the atomic number is decreased by one, and the mass number does not change. The n:p ratio consequently increases, and the daughter nuclide lies closer to the band of stability than the parent nuclide."
              }
            ],
            "id": "nkc7pa4e7vfm751",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "p4kw5m9orfqouuk",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "6aw1zft8o1apzsx",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/06/062eec8c42d029c9c43bf0474f8f2696/electron_capture.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": 550
          },
          {
